{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (NURSE): Right, obs are done before he comes in. Blood pressure's 128 over 78, pulse 76, temp 36.8, oxygen sat 98 percent. You can sit up on the bed.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (PATIENT): Umm, thanks. I kept my jumper half on because the waiting room was freezing and now it's warm, so if the pressure looks weird that's maybe why. Last winter at the GP it was higher, but that was a different day and I was rushing.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): She's narrated the whole drive from Brunswick, love. Every set of lights.",
  "[SEG0004] person_4 (DOCTOR): Sorry. Computer's crawling. Err. What brings you in?",
  "[SEG0005] person_2 (PATIENT): There's a lump on the left side of my neck. I've had it for three weeks. It doesn't hurt at all, which is the odd part, because I keep checking it morning and night and it's just... there.",
  "[SEG0006] person_4 (DOCTOR): Say that again? Wrong window. How long's the lump been there?",
  "[SEG0007] person_2 (PATIENT): Three weeks. Left side, under the jaw but a bit lower. Painless. I noticed it after a late close, I work at a cafe in Brunswick, and I thought it was a knot from staring down at the coffee machine all shift.",
  "[SEG0008] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): I told her in the first week to book. She reckoned it'd go.",
  "[SEG0009] person_4 (DOCTOR): Mm. Any fever?",
  "[SEG0010] person_2 (PATIENT): No fever now. I've been using mum's chemist thermometer, morning and night, and it's sat in the normal range every time. I was going to say chills too but I haven't had chills.",
  "[SEG0011] person_4 (DOCTOR): Night sweats? Or weight loss?",
  "[SEG0012] person_2 (PATIENT): Night sweats once. One night, ten days ago, I woke up damp and changed my shirt, and it hasn't happened again. Weight's the same, I weigh in because of the diabetes, same ballpark.",
  "[SEG0013] person_4 (DOCTOR): Of. Diabetes. What are you taking? Slowly, I'm typing.",
  "[SEG0014] person_2 (PATIENT): Umm, Metformine, 500 milligrams twice a day, breakfast and dinner, for the diabetes, since I was twenty-one. I'm twenty-four now. Mizartan 40 mg every morning for the pressure. And Omprazole 20 mg at night for the heartburn from all the coffee on early starts.",
  "[SEG0015] person_4 (DOCTOR): Mizartan? Repeat that, the screen's changed it.",
  "[SEG0016] person_2 (PATIENT): Mizartan. It's spelled M-i-z-a-r-t-a-n on the box, 40 milligrams, one every morning, for the pressure. I left the box on the kitchen bench, sorry.",
  "[SEG0017] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): And she stopped that antibiotic in March, tell him.",
  "[SEG0018] person_2 (PATIENT): Yeah, amoxicillin. I stopped it in March. It was for a sinus infection and my stomach went off, so I didn't finish the last few days. Not a rash, just nausea.",
  "[SEG0019] person_4 (DOCTOR): Did anyone give you something for this lump that you actually started?",
  "[SEG0020] person_2 (PATIENT): The other GP wrote prednisolone, a short course, but I never took it. I didn't even fill the script. I wanted someone to look at the lump before I swallowed steroids, in case it needed a picture not just tablets.",
  "[SEG0021] person_4 (DOCTOR): Any allergies? To medicines or anything else?",
  "[SEG0022] person_2 (PATIENT): No. No allergies. The amoxicillin thing was just my stomach, like I said.",
  "[SEG0023] person_4 (DOCTOR): Sore throat? Ear pain? Hurts when you swallow?",
  "[SEG0024] person_2 (PATIENT): No sore throat. No ear pain. Swallowing's fine, I had toast and tea this morning on purpose to check, which is probably overkill but I do that.",
  "[SEG0025] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Tell him about Miso. Go on.",
  "[SEG0026] person_2 (PATIENT): Right, our cat Miso scratched my left forearm about a month ago. It bled a little, I washed it, and it's healed now, just a faint line. I didn't think it linked to my neck, they're different spots, but mum's been reading things.",
  "[SEG0027] person_4 (DOCTOR): When was the scratch? Sorry, an email popped up.",
  "[SEG0028] person_2 (PATIENT): About a month ago. Left forearm. Healed. The lump started after that, the three weeks, so the scratch was first.",
  "[SEG0029] person_4 (DOCTOR): Okay. I was going to send you straight for a fine needle biopsy. With the cat scratch I'm changing that. Blood tests and an ultrasound of the neck, and we'll leave the biopsy.",
  "[SEG0030] person_2 (PATIENT): Oh. I had myself braced for a needle today, I even looked up whether you can drive after, which is silly because mum drove anyway.",
  "[SEG0031] person_4 (DOCTOR): Let me feel it. Chin up a bit. There's a soft lump on the left side of the neck, about two centimetres, it moves, it's not tender, and the skin over it looks normal. No other nodes that I can feel.",
  "[SEG0032] person_2 (PATIENT): About two centimetres, okay. I had one and a half on a sewing tape but I was squashing it in the bathroom mirror, so yours is the one that counts, I won't argue the half centimetre.",
  "[SEG0033] person_4 (DOCTOR): Mm. I'm not naming it today. It could be a reactive lymph node, possibly from the cat scratch, but I can't exclude other causes till the tests are back.",
  "[SEG0034] person_1 (NURSE): Want the bloods done now while she's here?",
  "[SEG0035] person_4 (DOCTOR): Yes. Full blood count, CRP, and cat scratch serology today, and book the neck ultrasound for this week. Come back in a fortnight for the results. If it suddenly gets bigger or you spike a fever, go to emergency rather than waiting.",
  "[SEG0036] person_2 (PATIENT): A fortnight works, I'll swap my Thursday close at the cafe, my manager's alright with notice. I'll bring the Mizartan box so you can see the spelling, and if the ultrasound is way out in the suburbs mum'll drive.",
  "[SEG0037] person_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER): I'll get her here. The vet thing for Miso is the week after, that's nothing to do with this."
 ],
